<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<paragraphs xml:id="para-extensive-and-intensive" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Relation between extensive and intensive properties</title>

    <assemblage>
      <title>:ember: Rule of Thumb</title>
      <p>
        If the value of a property is doubled when the amount of material comprising the system is doubled,
        then it is an <xref ref="def-extensive-property" text="custom">extensive property</xref>.
      </p>
      <p>
        If the value of a property is unchanged when the amount of material comprising the system is
        doubled, then it is an <xref ref="def-intensive-property" text="custom">intensive property</xref>
      </p>
    </assemblage>

    <remark>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Often times, <em>intensive properties</em> are constructed from ratios or derivatives of
          <em>extensive properties</em>.
        </p>
      </statement>


      <example>
        <p>
          Density (an <xref ref="def-intensive-property" text="custom">intensive property</xref>) is the ratio of mass to volume (both
          <xref ref="def-extensive-property" text="custom">extensive properties</xref>).
        </p>
        <md>
          \mathrm{density} = \frac{\mathrm{mass}}{\mathrm{volume}}
        </md>
      </example>
    </remark>

  </paragraphs>